  "worked_solution": "The sum formula for sine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(2x+\u03d5) & =R(\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(2x)\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\u03d5+\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(2x)\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\u03d5) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\u03d5\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(2x)+R\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\u03d5\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(2x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(2x)+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(2x)=R\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(2x+\u03d5),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(2x)+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(2x)=(R\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\u03d5)\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(2x)+(R\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\u03d5)\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(2x)\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(2x)$ and $\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(2x),$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\u03d5=1 \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\u03d5=\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\u03d5}{R\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\u03d5} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{1} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}\u2061\u03d5 & =\\sqrt{3} \\\\ \u03d5 & =60\\,{\\,}^{\u2218}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
